/* V77 — INK. The foundation of a print art direction, and the ground it prints on.

   ---- Why the game does not look good, stated exactly ----

   Photographed and traced through the drawing code, the fault is not the palette and it is not the
   creature designs. It is that **everything on screen has the same value structure.** The ground is
   dark, the figures are dark, and the only thing separating them is emitted light:

     - `v15Carapace` fills every creature with a gradient running trim → body → #0a1024 → #04060f,
       so every creature fades into the background at its own edges;
     - `v15Halo`, `v15Spark` and `v15Eye` are all `globalCompositeOperation='lighter'`;
     - `drawBackground` is a radial nebula plus additive stars plus concentric accent rings;
     - `V26_SKYLINES` paints #04070f towers with additive window lights.

   Five layers of one idea. The consequence is that the six archetypes have genuinely good, distinct
   silhouettes — that work was done and it was done well — and the player cannot see any of it,
   because each one is a dark body inside a coloured aura. **The aura is what reads, not the shape.**
   The art's real asset is being thrown away by its own treatment.

   Second fault: there is no ground. The six `worlds` are two-stop vertical gradients. A gradient is
   not a place; nothing in the frame has weight or surface.

   Third: hue is doing four jobs at once. `v15Palette` gives every archetype its own hue, so hue
   carries identity — while also carrying threat, ownership and UI state. A six-way hue code cannot
   be decoded at five taps a second.

   ---- What a visual identity actually is ----

   Across every game with a look you can name from a thumbnail, the identity is never the character
   design. It is **one process constraint applied to every pixel, with no exceptions.** Obra Dinn is
   1-bit dithering applied to a full 3D renderer. Cuphead is 1930s cel and watercolour including the
   paper tooth and the film grain. Downwell is three colours, total. Into the Breach is readability
   itself made into a style.

   This game currently has the opposite: a treatment (additive glow) applied to art that was
   designed for a different treatment.

   ---- The rule ----

        THE FIELD IS SCREENED. THE FIGURES ARE FLAT.

   Every background is built from halftone dots. Every figure is solid, uncut ink with a heavy key
   contour and no interior gradient, ever. That inverts the usual relationship — detailed characters
   over a soft background — and buys enormous figure/ground separation for nothing, because a flat
   shape on a dotted field is about the easiest thing a human visual system can find.

   It is also, deliberately, the exact opposite of what this game does today, and of what every
   other procedurally-drawn canvas game does. A screenshot of it is not mistakable for anything
   else, which is the whole brief.

   ---- Why this is the cheapest thing in the renderer, not the most expensive ----

   Flat fills and strokes over three pre-rendered tiles. No `createRadialGradient` per creature per
   frame, which is what ships today; no `shadowBlur`, which was profiled at 6.2ms of a 7.2ms frame
   before V15 removed it. And there is a hard fallback: every ink here is opaque over a known
   ground, so every `multiply` result can be precomputed as a literal constant and drawn
   `source-over` if a device ever profiles badly on blend modes. That is not true of the additive
   stack this replaces.

   ---- Switchable, on purpose ----

   `V77.on` turns the whole direction on and off in one place, and defaults OFF until every surface
   is converted. A print direction is worth nothing at 90% compliance: one additive glow left in the
   frame reads as a mistake rather than as a style, and a half-converted build looks worse than
   either of the two things it is between. The switch is what lets the conversion land one surface
   at a time without ever shipping the in-between. */

const V77={
 /* The master switch, and it is OFF, which is a deliberate call rather than an unfinished one.

    Four surfaces are converted and verified in the arena: this part's ground and screen, V78's
    cast, V79's deadline, kill effect and titan, and V80's chrome. Turned on and photographed, the
    arena is exactly what it should be.

    The home screen is not. V18 gave it its own root and its own procedural canvas (`#launchArt`)
    rather than the `.screen` / `.panel` structure every other screen uses, so it matches none of
    the selectors that converted the rest and it is the first thing anybody sees. Three attempts to
    reach it from CSS -- raising specificity, anchoring through body, blanking its children -- each
    fixed part of it and left the ground black, which means something in that subtree is painting
    the viewport from a place the stylesheet has not found yet.

    So the switch stays off, because the argument this whole part is built on applies to this part:
    a direction is worth nothing at ninety per cent, a half-converted build looks worse than either
    of the two things it is between, and the first screen a player sees is the worst possible place
    to be the exception. What is needed is one more pass that redraws `#launchArt` in ink and gives
    the home screen the same structure as the rest -- not another CSS override. Everything else is
    done and is one field away. */
 on:false,
 /* Six colours, five roles. Hue never encodes creature type: that job belongs entirely to
    silhouette, which is where the existing art is already strong. Red is "will kill you", blue is
    "yours", yellow is "the line". A code a player can read in 80ms. */
 paper:'#f3e9d6',   /* warm uncoated stock. the ground, everywhere */
 knock:'#fcf6e9',   /* bare paper punched back through ink. the only "white" */
 key:'#191411',     /* the key plate. warm near-black, never #000 */
 red:'#de3b26',     /* hostile ink -- riso fluorescent vermillion */
 blue:'#27478f',    /* player ink -- riso federal blue */
 yellow:'#f0ae22',  /* hazard: the deadline, warnings, wave progress. nothing else */
 green:'#3d9a67',   /* the rare fourth ink. pickups only, so it always means good news */
 /* The screen: dot pitch in CSS points. Fine enough to read as tone on a phone, coarse enough that
    the dots are visible -- and that visibility IS the identity. */
 pitch:4,
 /* How far the colour plate is offset from the key plate. Misregistration is the single strongest
    "this was printed" cue available and it costs two extra transforms. */
 slipX:1.7,
 slipY:1.4
};

/* Six worlds re-skin by swapping ONE value: the hostile ink, which is also the sky. Paper, key and
   hazard never change. Six distinct places that are unmistakably the same game. */
const V77_WORLDS=['#de3b26','#d6317e','#c97a18','#1f7a5e','#2e3a9e','#8e2230'];
function v77Ink(){return V77_WORLDS[game.world%V77_WORLDS.length];}

/* ---- A press has its own randomness, and it must not touch the game's ---------------------------

   Deliberately a separate stream from `rng()`. `rng()` advances `game.rng`, which is the seeded
   stream the spawner draws from: a drawing routine that borrowed it would make two players on the
   same daily seed meet different waves. And it must not be `Math.random()` either, because a
   hand-cut edge has to be the SAME hand-cut edge every frame or the creature boils. Seeded per
   shape, reset per call. */
let v77Seed=1337;
function v77Rnd(){v77Seed=(v77Seed*1664525+1013904223)&0x7fffffff;return v77Seed/0x7fffffff;}
function v77Seeded(n){v77Seed=(n|0)||1;}

/* ---- Pre-render ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Three offscreen canvases built once and rebuilt only on a resize, because the tiles are cut at
   device resolution so the dots land on whole pixels. Everything below is one fill or one
   drawImage per frame. */

let v77Cache=null,v77CacheDpr=0,v77CacheW=0;

function v77Paper(size){
 const c=document.createElement('canvas');
 c.width=c.height=size;
 const x=c.getContext('2d');
 x.fillStyle=V77.paper;
 x.fillRect(0,0,size,size);
 /* Grain, with less variance on the blue channel so the tooth reads warm rather than grey. */
 const img=x.getImageData(0,0,size,size),d=img.data;
 v77Seeded(7);
 for(let i=0;i<d.length;i+=4){
  const n=(v77Rnd()*2-1)*8;
  d[i]+=n;d[i+1]+=n*.95;d[i+2]+=n*.8;
 }
 x.putImageData(img,0,0);
 /* Fibre: short strokes, half of them darker than the sheet and half lighter. Uncoated stock is
    not smooth and a perfectly even ground is the thing that makes flat colour look like vector
    clip-art rather than like a print. */
 x.lineCap='round';
 for(let i=0;i<300;i++){
  const px=v77Rnd()*size,py=v77Rnd()*size,a=v77Rnd()*Math.PI,len=2+v77Rnd()*10;
  x.strokeStyle=v77Rnd()<.5?'rgba(25,20,17,.05)':'rgba(255,251,238,.12)';
  x.lineWidth=.5+v77Rnd()*.8;
  x.beginPath();x.moveTo(px,py);x.lineTo(px+Math.cos(a)*len,py+Math.sin(a)*len);x.stroke();
 }
 return c;
}

/* A 45-degree screen. The dot sits at (0,0) and (pitch,pitch) and is replicated at the tile edges
   so the lattice seams; radius from the square root of coverage, which is how a real screen
   converts tone to area. */
function v77HalftoneTile(frac,dpr){
 const pitch=V77.pitch*dpr;
 const s=pitch*2;
 const c=document.createElement('canvas');
 c.width=c.height=Math.round(s);
 const x=c.getContext('2d');
 x.fillStyle='#000';
 const r=Math.sqrt(frac)*pitch*.72;
 for(const [dx,dy] of [[0,0],[pitch,pitch]]){
  for(const [ox,oy] of [[0,0],[s,0],[0,s],[s,s],[-s,0],[0,-s]]){
   x.beginPath();x.arc(dx+ox,dy+oy,r,0,Math.PI*2);x.fill();
  }
 }
 return c;
}

/* The printed gradient -- bokashi, the ukiyo-e technique. Not a gradient: rows of dots whose radius
   falls off with a curve, so the transition is made of the same screen as everything else. One
   drawImage per frame. */
function v77Bokashi(w,h,colour,maxDot,gamma,dpr){
 const c=document.createElement('canvas');
 c.width=Math.round(w*dpr);c.height=Math.round(h*dpr);
 const x=c.getContext('2d');
 x.setTransform(dpr,0,0,dpr,0,0);
 x.fillStyle=colour;
 v77Seeded(91);
 const pitch=V77.pitch;
 for(let row=0,y=1;y<h;y+=pitch,row++){
  const t=1-y/h;
  const rad=Math.pow(t,gamma)*pitch*maxDot;
  if(rad<.18)continue;
  const off=(row%2)*pitch;
  for(let px=off-pitch;px<w+pitch;px+=pitch*2){
   x.beginPath();x.arc(px,y,rad*(.85+v77Rnd()*.3),0,Math.PI*2);x.fill();
  }
 }
 return c;
}

function v77Build(){
 const dpr=Math.max(1,Math.min(devicePixelRatio||1,isMobile?1.55:2));
 if(v77Cache&&v77CacheDpr===dpr&&v77CacheW===W)return v77Cache;
 const paper=ctx.createPattern(v77Paper(256),'repeat');
 const screens=[.1,.22,.4,.62].map((f)=>{
  const p=ctx.createPattern(v77HalftoneTile(f,dpr),'repeat');
  /* Pinned to device space so the dots do not swim when a figure rotates or scales. A real screen
     does not rotate with the artwork; that is what makes it read as a screen rather than as
     texture painted on the object. Cut at device resolution above so the pitch still measures
     four points on screen. */
  if(p.setTransform)p.setTransform(new DOMMatrix());
  return p;
 });
 v77CacheDpr=dpr;v77CacheW=W;
 v77Cache={dpr,paper,screens,sky:null,floor:null,skyInk:null};
 return v77Cache;
}

function v77Bands(){
 const cache=v77Build();
 const ink=v77Ink();
 if(cache.skyInk!==ink||!cache.sky){
  cache.skyInk=ink;
  cache.sky=v77Bokashi(W,Math.round(H*.56),ink,.66,2.2,cache.dpr);
  cache.floor=v77Bokashi(W,Math.round(H*.3),V77.blue,.58,2,cache.dpr);
 }
 return cache;
}

addEventListener('resize',()=>{v77Cache=null;},{passive:true});

/* ---- The rule, enforced rather than requested ------------------------------------------------

   Additive blending appears in twenty-six source files. Converting a surface at a time and trusting
   every future part to remember not to reach for `lighter` is how a direction dies: one glow left in
   the frame does not read as a leftover, it reads as a mistake, and this whole conversion is worth
   nothing at ninety per cent compliance.

   So the rule is enforced at the one place every caller has to pass through. While the direction is
   on, an additive composite mode is rewritten to `multiply` on the way in. A white-to-transparent
   glow multiplied over paper is a no-op, which is exactly what should happen to it; a coloured one
   becomes a flat tint, which is at worst harmless.

   Deliberately a property hook on the prototype and not a lint rule or a search-and-replace across
   twenty-six files. It is the smaller change, it is reversible with the switch, it cannot be
   forgotten by a part written next month, and it leaves every one of those call sites correct for
   the direction they were written for. The cost is honest and worth naming: this is a global patch
   on a platform object, so anything else on the page drawing to a 2D canvas is affected too. On
   this page nothing else does. */

(function(){
 const proto=typeof CanvasRenderingContext2D==='function'&&CanvasRenderingContext2D.prototype;
 if(!proto)return;
 const desc=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(proto,'globalCompositeOperation');
 if(!desc||!desc.set||!desc.get)return;
 const ADDITIVE={lighter:1,screen:1,'plus-lighter':1,'color-dodge':1};
 Object.defineProperty(proto,'globalCompositeOperation',{
  configurable:true,
  enumerable:desc.enumerable,
  get(){return desc.get.call(this);},
  set(v){desc.set.call(this,V77.on&&ADDITIVE[v]?'multiply':v);}
 });
})();

/* ---- Ink primitives ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The whole vocabulary. Every figure in the converted game is built from these four calls and
   nothing else -- no gradient, no shadow, no additive blend. Kept deliberately small: a large
   toolkit is how exceptions get made, and this direction is worth nothing with exceptions in it. */

/* A hand-cut edge. Every contour goes through this: vertices nudged by a deterministic per-shape
   jitter so nothing is machine-true, and the SAME jitter every frame so nothing boils. This one
   detail is most of the difference between "printed" and "vector clip-art", and it costs one
   pseudo-random draw per vertex. */
function v77Path(c,pts,jit,sd){
 v77Seeded(sd);
 c.beginPath();
 for(let i=0;i<pts.length;i++){
  const px=pts[i][0]+(v77Rnd()*2-1)*jit,py=pts[i][1]+(v77Rnd()*2-1)*jit;
  if(i)c.lineTo(px,py);else c.moveTo(px,py);
 }
 c.closePath();
}

/* Flat ink. `multiply` is the direction itself: two inks crossing make a third colour for free,
   exactly as overprinting does on press, and it is the structural inverse of `lighter` on dark. */
function v77Ink2(c,pts,col,sd,jit){
 c.save();
 c.globalCompositeOperation='multiply';
 c.fillStyle=col;
 v77Path(c,pts,jit===undefined?.8:jit,sd);
 c.fill();
 c.restore();
}

function v77Key(c,pts,weight,sd,jit){
 c.save();
 c.globalCompositeOperation='multiply';
 c.strokeStyle=V77.key;
 c.lineWidth=weight;
 c.lineJoin='round';
 c.lineCap='round';
 v77Path(c,pts,jit===undefined?.8:jit,sd);
 c.stroke();
 c.restore();
}

function v77Disc(c,x,y,r,col){
 c.save();
 c.globalCompositeOperation='multiply';
 c.fillStyle=col;
 c.beginPath();c.arc(x,y,r,0,Math.PI*2);c.fill();
 c.restore();
}

/* Bare paper punched back through ink. This replaces white everywhere -- eyes, visor slits, type.
   Drawn `source-over` rather than multiplied, because a knockout is the absence of ink and
   multiplying it would tint it with whatever is underneath. */
function v77Knock(c,pts,sd,jit){
 c.save();
 c.fillStyle=V77.knock;
 v77Path(c,pts,jit===undefined?.6:jit,sd);
 c.fill();
 c.restore();
}

/* Misregistration: the colour plate laid down a hair off the key plate. Two transforms, and the
   single strongest cue that a frame came off a press. */
function v77Slip(c,draw){
 c.save();
 c.translate(V77.slipX,V77.slipY);
 draw(true);
 c.restore();
 draw(false);
}

/* One wedge of screen inside a figure, and only one, so a shape gets form without ever getting a
   gradient. Clipped rather than filled to a path so the dots stay on the screen lattice. */
function v77Shade(c,pts,level,sd){
 const cache=v77Build();
 c.save();
 c.globalCompositeOperation='multiply';
 v77Path(c,pts,.8,sd);
 c.clip();
 c.fillStyle=cache.screens[Math.max(0,Math.min(3,level))];
 c.fillRect(-4,-4,W+8,H+8);
 c.restore();
}

/* ---- The ground -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The first surface converted, and the one that decides whether any of the rest is possible: paper,
   a printed sky, a printed floor, and the registration marks a press leaves in the margin. The
   overlap between the two bands makes a violet the palette never declares, which is what
   overprinting is for. */

/* Hooked on `drawBackground` rather than on `draw`.

   The first version wrapped `draw`, painted the sheet, and then called through — and the sheet was
   never seen for a single frame. The base `draw` opens by filling the whole canvas with the world's
   radial gradient before it calls anything, so the paper was being laid down and painted over
   immediately. Photographed, the creatures had converted and the ground had not, which looked like
   the switch was half-wired.

   `drawBackground` runs immediately after that fill and before anything else in the frame, so an
   override there covers the base gradient and nothing else. It also means the nebula, the additive
   stars, the planet and `v9WorldScenery` are all skipped in one place, which is the whole of the old
   ground. */

const v77Background=drawBackground;
drawBackground=function(w){
 if(!V77.on)return v77Background(w);

 const cache=v77Bands();

 ctx.save();
 ctx.setTransform(cache.dpr,0,0,cache.dpr,0,0);
 ctx.fillStyle=cache.paper;
 ctx.fillRect(0,0,W,H);
 ctx.globalCompositeOperation='multiply';
 ctx.drawImage(cache.sky,0,0,W,Math.round(H*.56));
 ctx.drawImage(cache.floor,0,Math.round(H-H*.3),W,Math.round(H*.3));
 ctx.restore();

 v77Registration();
};

/* Registration marks and the ink swatch strip: the marginalia of a printed sheet. Free identity --
   nobody else's game has them, they cost four strokes, and they say "this is a print" before a
   single creature has been looked at. */
function v77Registration(){
 const m=14,r=7;
 ctx.save();
 ctx.globalCompositeOperation='multiply';
 ctx.strokeStyle=V77.key;
 ctx.lineWidth=1;
 for(const [x,y] of [[m+r,V67.y()-4],[W-m-r,V67.y()-4],[m+r,H-m-r]]){
  ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(x,y,r,0,Math.PI*2);ctx.stroke();
  ctx.beginPath();ctx.moveTo(x-r-5,y);ctx.lineTo(x+r+5,y);ctx.stroke();
  ctx.beginPath();ctx.moveTo(x,y-r-5);ctx.lineTo(x,y+r+5);ctx.stroke();
 }
 const inks=[V77.key,v77Ink(),V77.blue,V77.yellow];
 for(let i=0;i<inks.length;i++){
  ctx.fillStyle=inks[i];
  ctx.fillRect(m+i*15,V67.y()+14,13,7);
 }
 ctx.restore();
}
